{
  "id": "08271_namaiki",
  "headword": "{生意気|なまいき}",
  "reading": "なまいき",
  "part_of_speech": "na-adjective",
  "gloss": "impudent; cheeky; cocky; saucy",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "impudent; cheeky; cocky; saucy",
      "explanation": "describes behavior or attitude that is presumptuous or disrespectful, especially from someone young or inexperienced toward their elders or superiors"
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "08271_namaiki_ex1",
      "japanese": "{生意気|なまいき}な{口|くち}をきくな。",
      "english": "Don't talk back to me.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "08271_namaiki_ex2",
      "japanese": "あの{子|こ}は{生意気|なまいき}だけど{憎|にく}めない。",
      "english": "That kid is cheeky, but you can't help liking them.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "08271_namaiki_ex3",
      "japanese": "{新人|しんじん}のくせに{生意気|なまいき}なことを{言|い}うな。",
      "english": "Don't be so cocky for a newcomer.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "USAGE:\n- {生意気|なまいき}な{口|くち}をきく (to talk back, be saucy)\n- {生意気|なまいき}を{言|い}う (to say impudent things)\n- {生意気|なまいき}なことを{言|い}う (to say cheeky things)\nOften used by adults about children or by seniors about juniors. The implication is that the person is behaving above their station or age.\nCan be used both critically and somewhat affectionately, depending on context. Sometimes used to describe a charming kind of precociousness.\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- {図々|ずうずう}しい (shameless, brazen)\n- {厚|あつ}かましい (impudent, shameless)\n- {口|くち}ごたえ (talking back)",
